[agent] ### How it was built (all on-platform)
[agent] 1. Created five feature groups (`transactions_fg`, `profiles_fg`, `activity_fg`, `account_health_fg`, `labels_fg`) with `event_time` set, and ingested the CSVs — both `transactions.csv` and `transactions_late.csv` into `transactions_fg`.
[agent] 2. Built a feature view with `labels_fg` as the PIT spine, LEFT-joining the four feature groups on `account_id`. The platform's feature-query engine performed the point-in-time-correct join.
[agent] 3. Consolidated the PIT-joined result into a single feature group `churn_features`, then created the final feature view `churntrainingc8f821` over that single group (no join) and materialized **training dataset version 1**.
[agent] ### One interface note
[agent] The CLI's `fv create` joins each feature group with `select_all` and offers no per-feature-group column projection. A multi-source join therefore always materializes each joined group's primary-key and event-time columns (and prefixes feature names like `transactions_fg_amount` when column names collide across groups). To deliver *exactly* the 9 requested columns, I had to run the PIT join through a feature view, then re-consolidate the platform-computed result into a single feature group and build the final feature view from that. A `select()`-style column projection in `fv create` would let the CLI produce the exact target schema in one step without the extra consolidation.
